<resources>
  <!--
    Light values. The -night copies carry the dark ones.

    These two follow dark mode so the splash hands over to the first screen without a flash,
    instead of always being dark.

    Which dark mode is the app's, not the phone's. The platform does build the starting window from
    the manifest theme before any of our code runs — but UiModeManager.setApplicationNightMode
    records this app's night mode with the system, so that resolution goes the way the Appearance
    setting asks. See applyAppNightMode; MainApplication sets it on every start and the setting
    itself sets it when it changes. Below Android 12 that hook does not exist and the starting
    window still follows the phone; themedAppContext covers the activity from attach onwards, so
    the mismatch is limited to the starting window rather than the first screen.
  -->
  <color name="splashscreen_background">#F5F4F0</color>
  <color name="activityBackground">#F5F4F0</color>

  <!--
    Not theme-aware, deliberately. This is the plate behind the launcher icon's negative space -
    the play triangle and the swoosh strokes are holes in the mark, and they read as the mark only
    against something dark. A launcher icon also does not follow the app's theme, so there is no
    handover to match here.
  -->
  <color name="iconBackground">#0d0d1a</color>
  <color name="colorPrimary">#023c69</color>
</resources>
